  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Very warm conditions today, with seasonable \ntemperatures an dry conditions for Halloween weekend. \n\nA weak cold front is expected to cross the area this morning into \nmidday bringing some spotty sprinkles to the highest ridges of the \nAlleghenies. Elsewhere, dry conditions will prevail with passing mid \nand high level cloud cover. High pressure will return later this \nafternoon and into the upcoming weekend ahead. The gradient between \nthe departing front and incoming high pressure will yield west to \nnorthwest downsloping winds up to 25 mph. \n\nExpect above normal temperatures as a result of the downsloping east \nof the mountains. Compressional heating from the Alleghenies, Blue \nRidge, and Catoctins should push highs in the low to mid 70s (60s \nmountains). Locations south of DC and down toward Charlottesville \nhave a shot to briefly touch 80 degrees given the very dry air ahead \nof and behind the front. \n\nCooler temperatures return tonight into the upcoming weekend. Expect \nmuch more seasonable weather for both Halloween Saturday and Sunday. \nHighs both days will push into the 60s with overnight lows in the \n30s and 40s. Expect more sunshine Saturday compared to Sunday given \nthe high nearby. Cloud cover will increase Sunday afternoon and \nnight as a shortwave trough passes south of the area. This trough \nmay touch off a shower or two Sunday night although most will remain \ndry given the residual mid-level dry air with high pressure nearby. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances will likely hold off until the second \nhalf of next week.\n\nModels are arriving at better agreement that mid level ridging will \nbuild along the east coast during the first half of next week. A \nshortwave trough may cross the area Monday before the ridge takes \nhold, and there is still an outside chance a few showers could occur \nas it passes. At a minimum, expect cloudier conditions. Temperatures \nwill likely climb Tuesday and Wednesday, with highs reaching the 70s \nfor many areas.\n\nA deep trough will approach from the west the second half of the \nweek. A low pressure system will likely be lurking over the Bahamas, \nand some guidance suggests this system, or at least some of its \nmoisture, could be swept northward into the Mid Atlantic ahead of \nthe trough. While the details remain uncertain, expect increasing \nrain chances in the Wednesday through Friday period.",
